First-write and clipped-native-window backing tests
==================================================

Run only on the audited native16K kernel through the established4K ABI launcher:
  cd /var/tmp/owned-test-dir
  userpages 4k ./first-write --native-16k
  userpages 4k ./clipped-firstwrite --native-16k

first-write: six fresh child arms (anonymous,shmem,regular file x dense/guarded),
eight threads, absent-PTE direct first writes, full untouched-byte verification,
DONTNEED and distinct-byte same-PTE refault racing, all source-file bytes verified.
Regular-file working directory MUST be non-tmpfs; otherwise that arm SKIPs.
All six must pass for qualification.4MiB useful payload/arm,<=16MiB native private
backing under a wasteful baseline plus source file.40s supervisor/15s child alarm
kills the fixture child/threads on timeout. No global pressure or kernel change.
This content/race fixture does not itself assert physical density.

clipped-firstwrite: root CAP_SYS_ADMIN pagemap visibility required.11 fresh child
arms cover all valid starts for1/2/3leaf windows,3+1tail fill and full4dense control;
two rounds with DONTNEED between. A single initial byte write must prefault every
neighbor in its clipped batch, proven by pagemap before any neighbor read. Full
zero/content checks then native PFN union enforce density.256groups/arm,
<=8MiB payload backing even on baseline;16MiB reserved VA.1MiB native slack bounds
pool sharding/background COW. Count3 alone allows natural16K/12K packing; mixed3+1
must fill tails.40s supervisor/8s child alarm, no GPU/reclaim/pressure.

The fixture metadata selftest (--selftest) checks tags/sorting only. It is not a
kernel-path test and does not replace running both fixtures on the target kernel.
